schema: track
object:
  title: Local Node Gathering Cadence
  type: track
  audience: local-node organizers (ReFi DAO / GreenPill Network chapter stewards)
  starting_context: starting or currently running a regen local node, deciding what to actually do when people show up
  outcome:
    - a sustainable weekly/monthly/quarterly gathering rhythm matched to the node's current stage
    - reduced organizer burnout from over-ambitious calendars
    - decisions made transparently enough that even people who disagree stay engaged
  concepts:
    - gathering-format-selection
    - consent-based-decision-making
    - node-burnout-prevention
  options:
    - community-meal
    - walk-and-talk
    - open-house
    - workshop
    - learning-circle
    - demonstration-day
    - town-hall
    - retrospective
    - milestone-party
    - open-celebration
  deployment_checks:
    - has the node named its current stage honestly (pre-formation / early formation / active operations / growth / renewal)?
    - is there a single owner for the recurring low-effort touchpoint (open house), not a committee?
    - are decision proposals circulated at least a week ahead of a town hall?
  failure_modes:
    - turning a relationship-building meal into a decision-making meeting ("while we're all here, let's just quickly...")
    - defaulting to lecture-mode for educational gatherings instead of hands-on/peer formats
    - running an ambitious high-effort calendar that burns out the host
    - skipping the informal-relationship layer and going straight to governance
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/types-of-gatherings.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: types-of-gatherings.md
  notes: >-
    Synthesized from the article's "Choosing the Right Format" stage table
    and rhythm guidance into a guided-pathway object (track schema), since
    the article itself functions as exactly that: a stage-matched sequence
    of options for a local node, not a single deployment.
